  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/bc/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_44.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "We find Tony Stark boarding a plane to Egypt to help a friend (named Paul) make the archaeological find of his career.&nbsp; Tony points out to his friend that Iron Man would be able to easily pinpoint the exact location of King Hatap's tomb, saving them perhaps a year of excavation.&nbsp; Sure enough, the next day, Iron Man not only locates the tomb but uses his diamond drills to penetrate the tough rock and digs them right inside the tomb.\n\nPaul explains that Hatap was called \"The Mad Pharaoh.\"&nbsp; His job done, Iron Man leaves, and returns later as Tony Stark, only to learn from Paul that mummy inside the sarcophagus has vanished!&nbsp; Stark agrees to remain behind with the supplies while Paul and his team go on in search of the stolen mummy.\n\nBut after everyone is gone, Hatap himself approaches Stark and indicates that, in fact, no one stole the mummy -- he simply walked out of the tomb!&nbsp; Stark doesn't believe that anyone could survive for 2000 years, so Hatap provides some background information.&nbsp; Back then, his rebellion against Cleopatra was defeated, so he drank a potion which made it look like he was dead.&nbsp; He was embalmed and entombed, but he really was in suspended animation, only to awaken 2000 years later.&nbsp; Now he plans to kidnap Stark and return to the past.&nbsp; To ensure Stark's cooperation, he's created a deadly disease to affect the tomb workers, who he promises to revive if Tony agrees to go with him.\n\nOnce Tony agrees, and the team cured, Hatap then summons the \"Chariot of Time,\" known only to ancient Egyptian sorcery!&nbsp; Upon their arrival in the past, Stark is able to switch to Iron Man.&nbsp; Hatap thinks the armored figure has killed Stark and flees, but Iron Man heads off to find Cleopatra.&nbsp; He thanks his lucky stars that his armor has a full charge.\n\nIron Man then finds a huge Roman legion laying siege to an Egyptian palace and races down to thwart the Romans, smashing things, which causes them to rush off, leaving Iron Man free to hunt for Cleopatra.&nbsp; Afterward, he talks to one of the Egyptian soldiers, who is able to explain where to look for her.\n\nHe finds her in a barge, about to be overtaken by Romans and swoops down to help.&nbsp; He gets an audience with her, and he's captivated by her beauty.&nbsp; Naturally, she too can speak perfect English, so they have no trouble at all communicating.&nbsp; She learns that Hatap is not dead, and Iron Man is sent off to find him (which he must do anyway since Hatap holds the charm required to return to the 20<sup>th</sup> century).\n\nAs Iron Man approaches the location where Hatap is, the Mad Pharaoh realizes that he can simply transport himself to 1963, \"safe from my enemies' swords!\"&nbsp; Iron Man easily makes his way to Hatap, who gets ready to use his charm to escape.&nbsp; But after Iron Man \"squirts a blob of oil\" at him, knocking it from his grasp, Hatap trips and impales himself on one of his own weapons!&nbsp; The rebellion is averted!\n\nCleopatra then arrives to say \"Oh, wondrous stranger, whoever you are, say you will remain and share my throne with me!&nbsp; For though I do not know your real identity...I, Cleopatra, have lost my heart to you!\"&nbsp; But Iron Man rubs the charm and fades away, heading back to 1963.\n\nDays later, at the archaeological dig, they unearth a mysterious hieroglyphic drawing of \"Cleopatra embracing a golden-armored figure!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A poor Iron Man outing with logic holes you could drive a Roman chariot through.\nThankfully, the backup story shows what an actual good idea looks like.",
  "subtitle": "The Mad \"Pharoah\"... and the Madder Plot",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | CoverTos44 | width=27%] The name \"[SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Pharoah]\" is misspelled boldly on the cover (should be \"Pharaoh\") and throughout the <i>entire book</i>.\n[BR]\n[BR] Later reprints correct this obvious mistake, which was featured in the 1980's <i>Marvel No-Prize Book</i>.&nbsp; Note also the screen shot above, which is taken directly from fandom.com's own entry on this issue, where they have elected to use the corrected version of the cover.\n[BR]\n[BR] But the image to the right shows a scan of the actual cover as it originally appeared.&nbsp; (This also highlights the fact that fandom's cover images aren't always of the actual books, because the background color of the entire scene is different as well.&nbsp; I like to read the originals wherever possible.)&nbsp; And the displayed title of the book in the summary box above also shows it [SPAN: severity=error; text=exactly as spelled in the issue].\n[BR]\n[BR] For the sake of my spell-checker, however, I have elected to type the word correctly throughout this review (and the plot summary as well).\n\n[DIVIDER]\nEven before King Hatap's tomb was discovered, I was a bit surprised at the cavalier attitude of Iron Man haphazardly drilling into an Egyptian structure (the \"excavation site\"), seemingly with <i>no regard whatsoever</i> for how fragile these ancient artifacts might be.&nbsp; It's almost as if the excavation crew should have been grateful he didn't bring a wrecking ball.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But <i>hey</i>, it was already page 4, and the story needed to get a move on!&nbsp; To blazes with <i>care</i>!)]\n\nAlmost from the start, there were <i>so many problems</i> with this story!&nbsp; I have no choice but to start enumerating them:\n\n[HANG0] Hatap has no problem speaking perfect English to Tony Stark when he finally reveals his presence!&nbsp; And in this case, Stark isn't even wearing his Iron Man armor in order to activate his Transistor-powered Universal Translator! [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(patent pending)].&nbsp; And lest we forget, everyone <i>else</i> in ancient Egypt <i>also</i> speaks English (naturally).\n\n[HANG0] If Hatap really was embalmed before being buried, I believe the custom was to use a prod to remove parts of the body's innards and bury them in a separate container in the coffin.&nbsp; Obviously this did not happen, or he would surely not have survived.\n[BR]\n[BR] In fairness, however, Paul, the archaeologist, does explicitly mention at the top of page 5 that \"Hatap must've had his own peculiar ideas about embalming!\"&nbsp; This at least <i>suggests</i> that it was not done in the traditional manner.\n\n[HANG0] Though Hatap left instructions for Cleopatra on how he wanted himself \"embalmed and entombed,\" it took a lot of trust on his part that she would follow them, especially as he had just led a rebellion against her.&nbsp; Why would Cleopatra go to all that trouble for someone who was attempting her overthrow?&nbsp; She even created an entire tomb for him!&nbsp; That's <i>awfully magnanimous</i> of her!&nbsp; (It's hard to imagine any ruler rewarding a traitor with such deluxe burial accommodations.)\n\n[HANG0] Page 6 indicates that Hatap has learned of Stark and how brilliant he is.&nbsp; <b><i>Oh?</i></b>  Exactly <i>when</i> did he learn this?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=The guy just woke up from a 2000-year-old sleep, not to mention having had to somehow worm his way out of his mummy wrappings!]&nbsp; One <i>easily</i> imagines this taking far longer than the story allows.\n[ASSET: center | HatapsCaptive | width=50%]\n\n[HANG0] In the past, after Stark becomes Iron Man, Hatap believes the \"flying monster has <i>slain</i> him!\"&nbsp; He runs off, but did he ever pause to think about how his situation has <i>not improved in the slightest</i>?&nbsp; He took such great pains to survive for 2000 years, then kidnaps the brilliant Tony Stark whose \"skills will be invaluable to me,\" but when Stark is later removed from the action, he simply moves ahead once more with his rebellion without any help -- the very help he insisted was essential!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=What was the whole point of all this then?]&nbsp; How have things improved for him?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Idiot!)]\n\n[ASSET: right | InterferingWithThePast | width=12%] [HANG0] Iron Man sends an entire Roman army on the run and never once even considers how his presence in the past might alter the future.&nbsp; I mean, [SPAN: severity=error; text=it <i>never once</i> crosses his mind!]&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: I'd ask if Stark had ever watched any <i>Star Trek</i> episodes at all, but then again, that is still more than three years in the future when this story was released.&nbsp; Did he at least watch the <i>Twilight Zone</i>?]\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Shame on you, Stark!</i>\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | BlobOfOil | width=12%] [ASSET: right | WondrousStranger | width=28%] The [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved], thanks to a squirt of oil and an upturned sword (the Comics Code Authority spares us from the moment of actual impalement).\n[BR]\n[BR] And though Cleopatra embraces the \"wondrous stranger\" and begs him to \"share my throne\" (after only a single encounter -- <i>surely </i>a record even by comic‑book romance standards), Iron Man has to return to 1963 if for no other reason than to recharge (to remain alive).\n\n[ASSET: left | MysteriousHieroglyph | width=12%] And in a final, gag-worthy moment, Stark is present when his friend Paul (apparently no longer worried about the mystery of the missing mummy), discovers a puzzling hieroglyph of\n[BR]\n[BR] \"Cleopatra embracing a golden-armored figure!\"\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | HeckArtwork | width=38%] Finally, I must say that I thought Don Heck's artwork quite good.&nbsp; His depiction of Cleopatra on the splash page immediately sets the scene for how well his ultra-fine lines would portray her throughout the entire episode.&nbsp; Despite the fact that the story itself was substandard, one cannot say the same about the art.&nbsp; Heck seems an <i>excellent</i> fit for this character.\n[BR]\n[BR] And while I'm on the subject of art, Heck seems to have decided that the antenna on Iron Man's left shoulder (that Jack Kirby kept drawing in his outings) is okay to keep, but at a far more subdued length than the one Kirby kept drawing.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even so, Heck keeps forgetting to draw it in some panels (<i>most</i> of them, actually), just like Kirby did.&nbsp; (<i>Hey</i>, it's almost a <i>tradition</i> at this point!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis is poor storytelling with indefensible elements.&nbsp; Its problems are simply too numerous to overlook (even once one gets past the blatant spelling error repeated from cover to final story page).\n\nThe Mad Pharaoh wasn't the only one who was mad -- the writer of this story must also have been mad (<i>sorry, Stan</i>) to think we were going to <i>accept</i> any of this as passing for a somewhat-believable tale!&nbsp; You couldn't even spell the villain's <i>name</i> correctly!&nbsp; (It's hard to build menace when the typography is working against you.)\n\nAnd even if that was indeed the scriptwriter's fault for perhaps typing it incorrectly, one would at least expect that letterer, Sam Rosen, might at least have checked it in a dictionary.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=Alas, no!]\n\nThere are other historical inaccuracies with this tale as well, but to list them out would only be adding insult to injury.&nbsp; Suffice it to say this episode, far from being a worthy Iron Man episode, only seems to help underscore how ridiculous some of his adventures have been so far.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=One can only hope that the creators improve this title in the coming months...]\n\n<b>Final Note:</b>\n[BR] This issue got a half-point boost in the final rating only because of the [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"One of Us Must Die!\"] secondary story -- which was quick and to the point (despite being set in 1998 on an intergalactic \"luxury space yacht,\" <i>heh</i>) and was surprisingly clever for a short story, being Twilight Zone-like in its twist ending.\n\nVery nice!&nbsp; I enjoyed it [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(far more than the title story)].\n[BR] It was the only hint of a good idea to be found.",
  "decoder_ring": "For more on Cleopatra, be sure to check out <i>Strange Tales</i> #124 (ST #124), published about a year later.",
  "highlight": "Iron Man takes a wild detour into ancient Egypt, but the plot (and lack of a dictionary) are the real villains."
}